{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8c87a86fa0c0f496","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The complex is a d⁹ Cu(II) system in an octahedral field. However, Cu(II) complexes are typically Jahn-Teller distorted, leading to elongated octahedra with two long axial bonds and four shorter equatorial bonds. This distortion splits the d-orbitals further, and the standard octahedral Tanabe-Sugano analysis does not directly apply.\n\nThe observed spectrum shows a single broad, asymmetric band with a maximum at 14,720 cm⁻¹ and a shoulder at 12,240 cm⁻¹. These features are characteristic of Jahn-Teller distorted Cu(II) complexes, where the d⁹ configuration leads to a ground state that is a doublet (²E_g), and the excited states are split due to distortion.\n\nThe Tanabe-Sugano model for d⁹ is not standard in the same way as for d²–d⁸, and the analysis is complicated by the Jahn-Teller distortion. While the tool `mcp__tanabesugano__ts_fit_spectrum` can be used for d⁹, the presence of Jahn-Teller distortion means that the energy levels are not well described by the idealized octahedral model. Therefore, fitting Dq and B parameters using the standard Tanabe-Sugano framework is not reliable.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"Jahn-Teller distortion in d9 Cu(II) complexes invalidates the standard octahedral ligand-field model.\"}"}],"model":"qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5193,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c986205f-e608-463b-af56-3304b3ce6534","uuid":"c43c4725-64a4-4f32-a844-7c3779043711","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:46:31.396Z"}
